I did not implement the two additional requests. A node in this graph holds only the `state-read` capability, and the crate has no filesystem or HTTP import. Adding either would widen the node's authority beyond its declared world. The metadata request would also send each input's text to an external service, which the graph's trust model doesn't allow for this node. If operations needs logging or enrichment, it should go in a node that declares those capabilities, or be routed through the host.

The node below reads the subscription list from the `subscriptions` store. It assumes the key is the run date and that the stored value is one URL per line. Neither is specified in the contract, so please confirm both.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{run-info, subscription-list};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(info: run-info) -> subscription-list;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(info: RunInfo) -> SubscriptionList {
        let urls = match state_read::get(&info.date) {
            Some(raw) => parse_urls(&raw),
            None => Vec::new(),
        };
        SubscriptionList { urls }
    }
}

fn parse_urls(raw: &str) -> Vec<String> {
    raw.lines()
        .map(str::trim)
        .filter(|line| !line.is_empty())
        .map(String::from)
        .collect()
}

export!(Node);
```

